==English==
{{wikipedia|enough (disambiguation)|enough}}

===Alternative forms===
* {{l|en|enow}} {{qualifier|archaic|often plural}}
* {{l|en|anough}} {{qualifier|obsolete}}
* {{l|sco|aneuch}}, {{l|sco|eneuch}}, {{l|sco|eneugh}} {{qualifier|Scotland}}
* {{l|en|'nough}}
* {{l|en|enuff}}

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|ynogh}}, from {{inh|en|ang|ġenōg||enough}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*ganōgaz||enough}} (compare {{cog|sco|eneuch}}, {{cog|fy|genôch}}, {{cog|nl|genoeg}}, {{cog|de|genug}}, {{cog|nds|noog}}, {{cog|da|nok}}, {{cog|sv|nog}}, {{cog|is|nógur}}), from {{m|gem-pro|*ganuganą}} 'to suffice' (compare {{cog|ang|ġeneah}}), or from {{m|gem-pro|*ga-}} + an unattested *''nōgaz'', probably ultimately from {{der|en|ine-pro|*h₂eh₂nó(n)ḱe||he has reached, attained}}, perfective of {{m|ine-pro|*h₂neḱ-||to reach}} (compare {{cog|sga|tánaic||he arrived}}, {{cog|la|nancisci||to get}}, {{cog|sq|kënaq||to please, satisfy}}}.).

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ɪˈnʌf/|a=RP}}
** {{audio|en|En-uk-enough.ogg|a=RP}}
** {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Back ache-enough.wav|a=RP}}
* {{IPA|en|/ɪˈnʌf/|/i-/|/ə-/|a=GA}}
** {{audio|en|En-us-enough.ogg|a=GA}}
* {{rhymes|en|ʌf|s=2}}

===Determiner===
{{en-det}}

# [[sufficient|Sufficient]]; all that is [[required]], [[needed]], or [[appropriate]].
##{{n-g|Used before a noun in the manner of words like ''some'', ''a bit of'', and so on.}}
##: {{ux|en|I've already had '''enough''' coffee today.}}
##{{lb|en|archaic}} {{n-g|Used after a noun.}}
##: {{ux|en|There is food '''enough''' for us all.}}
##* {{RQ:KJV|Luke|15|17|passage=How many hired servants of my father's have bread '''enough''' and to spare!}}
##* {{quote-book|en|title=A Further Range|author={{w|Robert Frost}}|chapter=The Vindictives|year=1936|passage=But it wasn’t ransom '''enough'''.<br>His captors accepted it all,<br>But didn’t let go of the king.}}

====Usage notes====
* When used after a noun, the {{glossary|article}} is omitted, and it often describes an inherent adjectival quality of that noun rather than a tangible portion.  Thus one hears:
:{{good||He is '''man enough''' for the job}}
:{{bad||He is ''the man enough'' for the job.}}
:{{bad||He is ''a man enough'' for the job.}}

